{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And Balak son of Zippor saw all that Israel had done to the Amorite.</b> But surely the miracles of Egypt were greater than these — so why did he not reflect upon those great and awesome deeds that HaShem had performed? And further, one must wonder that Israel is mentioned here by four names: Israel, the people, the children of Israel, the congregation. For first it says, ״אֵת כָּל אֲשֶׁר עָשָׂה יִשְׂרָאֵל לָאֱמֹרִי״, and afterward it says, ״וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי הָעָם״, and afterward it says, ״וַיָּקָץ מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, and afterward it says, ״עַתָּה יְלַחֲכוּ הַקָּהָל״. Is this not surprising?" "And the explanation of the matter is thus: it is the way of the world that kings possess a book of chronicles, in which are recorded all the events from many days past. But the mass of common people know only the present — what they see with their eyes or what they hear by rumor of the events in their own time. Therefore Balak, who was a king — behold, from many days past there would be available to him what Jacob Israel the grandfather had done to the Amorite, for he was only one man and his household of numbered souls, and even so he defeated an entire nation, as it is written (Genesis 48:22): ״אֲשֶׁר לָקַחְתִּי מִיַּד הָאֱמֹרִי בְּחַרְבִּי וּבְקַשְׁתִּי״. And according to the opinion of our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Batra 123a), who explained ״בְּחַרְבִּי וּבְקַשְׁתִּי״ to mean 'by his prayer,' it fits well: for Balak saw all that Israel the grandfather had done to the Amorite by his prayer, since all his strength was in his mouth. Therefore he too sought out a man whose strength was likewise in his mouth. And therefore it says ״וַיַּרְא בָּלָק״ and not ״וַיִּשְׁמַע בָּלָק״, for all those events — behold, they are written in the book of chronicles of the kings of the earth, and he saw with his own eyes the matter written in the book, and dread entered his heart, saying: if this is what they did in earlier years when they were but numbered souls, what will they do now when they are a great and mighty people? And therefore it does not say ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ or ״הָעָם״ or ״קָהָל״, because he is speaking of Israel the grandfather." "But Moab — that is, the general mass of the people — did not reflect on what had passed, for they did not know of it, and they feared only the present, what they saw with their eyes: a people great and mighty. Therefore it says, ״וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי הָעָם כִּי רַב הוּא״. And the common people of Moab feared two groups, and this fear was not like that fear: for because of the mixed multitude they feared on account of their sheer numbers, for it is found in the Midrash that the mixed multitude was twice sixty myriads, and concerning them it says, ״וַיָּגָר מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי הָעָם כִּי רַב הוּא״ — he called the mixed multitude by the name ״הָעָם״, for they are not of the children of Israel; ״כִּי רַב הוּא״ — for they are truly many. But the children of Israel, who were not so numerous — they did not fear them on account of their numbers. However, from another direction there was the dread of Israel upon them, concerning which it says, ״וַיָּקָץ מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, as we explained in the portion of Shemot (Exodus 1:12) on ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״: the meaning is not that Israel became as thorns in their eyes — if so, it should have said ״וַיָּקֻצוּ בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״; what is the meaning of ״מִפְּנֵי״? Rather the reverse is more plausible: that the Egyptians likened themselves to thorns in comparison with the children of Israel, who were likened to a vine, as it is written (Psalms 80:9–10): ״גֶּפֶן מִמִּצְרַיִם תַּסִּיעַ, תְּגָרֵשׁ גּוֹיִם וַתִּטָּעֶהָ. פִּנִּיתָ לְפָנֶיהָ וַתַּשְׁרֵשׁ שָׁרָשֶׁיהָ וַתְּמַלֵּא אָרֶץ״. For it is customary in the world, when the owner of a field wishes to plant a vineyard in a field full of thorns, that he uproots the thorns from the root and casts them away, in order to plant the vine in their place. So too the Egyptians feared that Israel would be planted in the land like a vine, and they would be uprooted from the land like a discarded thorn, all of them (II Samuel 23:6), as it is written ״וְעָלָה מִן הָאָרֶץ״ (Exodus 1:10) — ״וְעָלִינוּ״ should have been the expression, and so forth. Therefore it says, ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that they would be shaken from the land because of the children of Israel, who were likened to a vine. So too here it says, ״וַיָּקָץ מוֹאָב מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that they feared lest Moab be cast out like a thorn from the land because of the children of Israel, because of the vine that HaShem had transplanted from Egypt to drive out nations from before them and to plant them there. Therefore it says ״בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״, for Israel the grandfather is no longer, for he has already gone to his world and his sons will come there; but not the mixed multitude, who are called by the name ״הָעָם״, for what is the preeminence of the mixed multitude over the other nations — that He would drive out nations before them and plant them in their place — if not along with the children of Israel?" "And afterward it says, ״וַיֹּאמֶר מוֹאָב אֶל זִקְנֵי מִדְיָן עַתָּה יְלַחֲכוּ הַקָּהָל״ and so forth. And what did the Midianites see to involve themselves in a quarrel that was not theirs? Rather, according to what we find that Rashi explained in the portion of Matot (Numbers 31:6), ״אוֹתָם וְאֶת פִּינְחָס״ — that he went to avenge the vengeance of Joseph his mother's father, as it is written (Genesis 37:36): ״וְהַמְּדָנִים מָכְרוּ אוֹתוֹ לְפוֹטִיפַר״, and the sons of Joseph are called ״קָהָל״, as it is written (Genesis 35:11): ״גּוֹי וּקְהַל גּוֹיִם״ — Rashi explained that ״קְהַל גּוֹיִם״ is said of Joseph. And so the elders of Moab said to Midian: you too have reason to fear the congregation — the sons of Joseph — who will wish to take from you vengeance for Joseph their father, the firstborn ox. This is what it says: ״עַתָּה יְלַחֲכוּ הַקָּהָל אֶת כָּל סְבִיבוֹתֵינוּ כִּלְחֹךְ הַשּׁוֹר״, for ״הַקָּהָל״ — the sons of Joseph, the firstborn ox — they are; therefore they will lick us like an ox that licks, in order to take the vengeance of the ox. And this is a precious interpretation."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {2 "The Aramaic parentheticals מַה גְּבַר מִגֻּבְרִין (Sanhedrin 65a) and וְעָלִינוּ מִבָּעֵי לֵיהּ are rendered in their original form as the Kli Yakar cites them, following the instruction that quoted material stays in its original language."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:42:46.789051806Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}